{% comment %}
  The reviews UI itself, shared by both entry points.

  Cited ships two blocks that render exactly the same thing:

    blocks/reviews.liquid       — an app block. The merchant places it inside
                                  their product section. Exact placement, and
                                  no JavaScript is involved at any point.
    blocks/reviews-embed.liquid — an app embed. One toggle in Theme settings
                                  and it is on for every product, with no
                                  placement step and nothing a theme can
                                  refuse. Shopify injects it at the end of
                                  <body>, so a small script moves it into
                                  position.

  They live in one snippet because they had already started to drift: `author`
  on the standard metaobject is a customer reference, not a name, and fixing
  that in one copy while the other kept writing a person's name into a
  reference field is precisely the bug that made syndication fail silently for
  every review ever submitted.

  Parameters are passed explicitly — `render` does not inherit outer scope —
  so this snippet cannot accidentally depend on which block called it.

  Data source: the standard `product_review` metaobject, plus the
  Shopify-mandated `reviews.rating` / `reviews.rating_count` product
  metafields that approved review apps must maintain.

  CSS is scoped entirely under .cited-reviews and driven by custom properties.
  There are NO global selectors and NO element resets — the most common
  competitor complaint is a review widget breaking the theme's buy buttons
  (PLAN.md §2, W1). This cannot reach outside itself.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign rating_field = product.metafields.reviews.rating
  assign count_field = product.metafields.reviews.rating_count

  assign rating_avg = 0
  if rating_field != blank and rating_field.value != blank
    assign rating_avg = rating_field.value.rating | times: 1.0
  endif

  assign rating_count = 0
  if count_field != blank
    assign rating_count = count_field.value | plus: 0
  endif

  # Shopify's `reviews` namespace holds ONLY rating and rating_count - there is
  # no product-to-reviews link in it. This previously read
  # `reviews.product_reviews`, a path that does not exist on any store, so the
  # block could render an average and never a single review.
  #
  # Cited maintains the link itself: an app-owned list of product_review
  # metaobject references, newest first, kept in step by the syndication job.
  # `app` is the theme-app-extension alias for this app's reserved namespace.
  # The app's reserved namespace, as LIQUID sees it.
  #
  # `$app` in the Admin API resolves to `app--{app_id}` on the shop, and that
  # is the only accessor that works here. `product.metafields.app.reviews` —
  # the form the documentation describes and the form this block used — returns
  # blank, verified on a live storefront against a definition that exists with
  # storefront=PUBLIC_READ and a value of size 2. That one wrong accessor is
  # why the block rendered an aggregate above an empty review list on every
  # store, and why AI summaries and the video upload input never appeared.
  #
  # The id is this app's and is fixed. `app` is still tried as a fallback, so
  # if the documented alias does resolve somewhere, that path keeps working.
  assign cited_ns = 'app--410130251777'

  assign reviews_list = product.metafields[cited_ns].reviews.value
  if reviews_list == blank
    assign reviews_list = product.metafields.app.reviews.value
  endif
  # Same reserved namespace as the review list above. Written by the AI summary
  # job for PRO stores only; absent everywhere else, which is why the render
  # below is guarded rather than conditional on any plan flag reaching Liquid.
  assign review_summary = product.metafields[cited_ns].summary.value
  if review_summary == blank
    assign review_summary = product.metafields.app.summary.value
  endif
  # The 1–5 histogram behind the breakdown panel, written by the aggregate job
  # from counts it already had. Absent on a store that has not resynced since
  # the field existed, which is why the panel below is guarded rather than
  # assumed.
  assign rating_breakdown = product.metafields[cited_ns].breakdown.value
  if rating_breakdown == blank
    assign rating_breakdown = product.metafields.app.breakdown.value
  endif

  # Shop-level feature flags, written by the app when the plan changes. Liquid
  # has no way to ask what plan a store is on — there is no request to our
  # server in this path, which is the point of a server-rendered block — so
  # anything gated by plan has to arrive as data Shopify already holds.
  #
  # This decides what the form OFFERS. The proxy route decides what it ACCEPTS,
  # and that is the check that actually enforces anything: a theme edited to
  # restore the input gets a rejected upload, not a free feature.
  assign cited_features = shop.metafields[cited_ns].features.value
  if cited_features == blank
    assign cited_features = shop.metafields.app.features.value
  endif

  # ── Appearance, chosen in the app ──
  #
  # These used to be block settings, which meant every theme, template and
  # duplicated block carried its own copy: a merchant changed their star colour
  # in one place and the rating beside the price stayed yellow. One shop-level
  # record now drives every Cited block.
  #
  # Each value falls back to what the caller passed, so a store that has never
  # opened the customiser renders exactly as before and nothing depends on the
  # metafield existing.
  assign ws = shop.metafields[cited_ns].widget_settings.value
  if ws == blank
    assign ws = shop.metafields.app.widget_settings.value
  endif

  if ws != blank
    assign star_color = ws.star_color | default: star_color
    assign text_color = ws.text_color | default: text_color
    assign muted_color = ws.muted_color | default: muted_color
    assign border_color = ws.border_color | default: border_color
    assign corner_radius = ws.corner_radius | default: corner_radius
    assign spacing = ws.spacing | default: spacing
    assign heading = ws.heading | default: heading
    assign empty_text = ws.empty_text | default: empty_text
    assign anonymous_label = ws.anonymous_label | default: anonymous_label
    assign form_heading = ws.form_heading | default: form_heading
    assign form_button = ws.form_button | default: form_button
  endif
  assign video_enabled = false
  if cited_features.video
    assign video_enabled = true
  endif

  assign per_page = per_page | default: 10

  # `| floor`, not `| plus: 0`. The rating metafield stores scale_max as "5.0",
  # and `plus` yields the FLOAT 5.0 — which Liquid renders as "out of 5.0" and,
  # far worse, cannot be used as a range bound: `(1..5.0)` calls Integer("5.0"),
  # which raises "invalid integer". The star loop then emitted nothing at all,
  # so every rating on every storefront rendered with no stars.
  assign scale_max = 5
  if rating_field != blank and rating_field.value.scale_max != blank
    assign scale_max = rating_field.value.scale_max | floor
  endif
  if scale_max < 1
    assign scale_max = 5
  endif

  assign has_reviews = false
  if rating_count > 0
    assign has_reviews = true
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  ── What the gallery and the filter chips need to know ──

  Computed once, before anything renders, because all three answers are needed
  ABOVE the list they describe: the photo strip sits at the top, and a filter
  chip that hides every review is worse than no chip, so "are there any
  reviews with video?" has to be answered before the chip is drawn.

  Liquid has no arrays to push onto, so the gallery is accumulated as a
  delimited string and split back. `|` cannot occur in a Shopify CDN URL, which
  is what makes it safe as a separator.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign gallery_raw = ''
  assign any_photos = false
  assign any_videos = false

  if show_media and reviews_list != blank
    for review in reviews_list limit: per_page
      for media_url in review.media_urls
        assign is_video = false
        if media_url contains '/cdn/shop/videos/' or media_url contains '.mp4' or media_url contains '.webm' or media_url contains '.mov'
          assign is_video = true
        endif
        if is_video
          assign any_videos = true
        else
          assign any_photos = true
          assign gallery_raw = gallery_raw | append: media_url | append: '|'
        endif
      endfor
    endfor
  endif

  assign gallery = ''
  if gallery_raw != blank
    assign gallery = gallery_raw | split: '|'
  endif

  # How many cards this page actually renders, bucketed to 1, 2 or many.
  #
  # `auto-fit` collapses the tracks nothing lands in, so it never leaves an
  # empty column — but with one review it hands that single card the entire
  # width, and a 70rem-wide review card is its own kind of broken. The count
  # is known here, so the layout does not have to infer it: one card gets a
  # readable maximum, two share the row, three or more flow.
  assign shown_count = 0
  if reviews_list != blank
    assign shown_count = reviews_list.size
    if shown_count > per_page
      assign shown_count = per_page
    endif
  endif

  assign list_bucket = 'many'
  if shown_count == 1
    assign list_bucket = 'one'
  elsif shown_count == 2
    assign list_bucket = 'two'
  endif

  # The store-wide digest, for the second tab. Absent until the worker has
  # published one, which is why the tab bar below is conditional rather than
  # assumed — a tab that opens an empty panel is worse than one tab.
  assign store_digest = shop.metafields[cited_ns].digest.value
  if store_digest == blank
    assign store_digest = shop.metafields.app.digest.value
  endif
  assign store_reviews = store_digest.reviews
  assign has_store = false
  if store_reviews != blank and store_reviews.size > 0
    assign has_store = true
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  Styles live in assets/, not in a "stylesheet" tag: that tag is a theme
  *section* feature and the extension bundler rejects it in an app block
  ("Cannot use stylesheet tag in app blocks"). Emitting the link here also
  means the stylesheet is requested before the markup below it parses, so it
  still paints without a flash of unstyled content.
{%- endcomment -%}
{{ 'cited-reviews.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}


{%- comment -%}
  The merchant's palette, captured once so it can be stamped on BOTH the
  section and the rating summary.

  The summary is relocated out of this section on the embed path — it belongs
  under the product title, and the rest of the block belongs further down the
  page — and every colour here is a custom property inherited from the section
  it would be leaving. Carrying its own copy makes it self-sufficient wherever
  it lands, rather than falling back to the stylesheet defaults and quietly
  ignoring the colours the merchant chose.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- capture cited_vars -%}
--cited-star: {{ star_color }};
--cited-text: {{ text_color }};
--cited-muted: {{ muted_color }};
--cited-border: {{ border_color }};
--cited-radius: {{ corner_radius }}px;
--cited-gap: {{ spacing }}px;
{%- endcapture -%}

<section
  class="cited-reviews"
  id="cited-reviews-{{ uid }}"
  data-cited-product="{{ product.id }}"
  data-cited-per-page="{{ per_page }}"
  {% if is_embed %}
    data-cited-embed
    data-cited-anchor="{{ anchor | escape }}"
    data-cited-title="{{ product.title | escape }}"
  {% endif %}
  style="{{ cited_vars }}"
  {{ shopify_attributes }}
>
  {%- comment -%}
    ── Tabs, filters and sort, with no JavaScript ──

    Radio inputs, visually hidden, as DIRECT children of the section. That
    placement is the whole trick: `~` reaches only siblings, so state kept here
    can style the layout, the panels and every card inside them, while the
    labels that operate it sit wherever they look right.

    The CLASS carries the meaning and the `name` carries the uid. Ids would
    have forced the rules into inline CSS — a stylesheet cannot know a block's
    uid — whereas classes let all of this live in assets/ and stay cacheable.
    The uid on `name` is still essential: two Cited blocks on one page must not
    share a radio group, or filtering one silently reorders the other.

    Sorting is real and is done with `order`, not faked. What is NOT offered is
    "most helpful": the standard product_review metaobject carries no
    helpfulness signal, so that control would be a label over an arbitrary
    order.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if has_reviews -%}
    {%- if has_store -%}
      <input class="cited-state cited-state--tab-product" type="radio" name="cited-tab-{{ uid }}" id="cited-tab-product-{{ uid }}" checked>
      <input class="cited-state cited-state--tab-store" type="radio" name="cited-tab-{{ uid }}" id="cited-tab-store-{{ uid }}">
    {%- endif -%}

    <input class="cited-state cited-state--f-all" type="radio" name="cited-f-{{ uid }}" id="cited-f-all-{{ uid }}" checked>
    {%- if any_photos -%}
      <input class="cited-state cited-state--f-photo" type="radio" name="cited-f-{{ uid }}" id="cited-f-photo-{{ uid }}">
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- if any_videos -%}
      <input class="cited-state cited-state--f-video" type="radio" name="cited-f-{{ uid }}" id="cited-f-video-{{ uid }}">
    {%- endif -%}

    <input class="cited-state cited-state--r-all" type="radio" name="cited-r-{{ uid }}" id="cited-r-all-{{ uid }}" checked>
    {%- for i in (1..5) -%}
      <input class="cited-state cited-state--r{{ i }}" type="radio" name="cited-r-{{ uid }}" id="cited-r-{{ i }}-{{ uid }}">
    {%- endfor -%}

    <input class="cited-state cited-state--s-new" type="radio" name="cited-s-{{ uid }}" id="cited-s-new-{{ uid }}" checked>
    <input class="cited-state cited-state--s-high" type="radio" name="cited-s-{{ uid }}" id="cited-s-high-{{ uid }}">
    <input class="cited-state cited-state--s-low" type="radio" name="cited-s-{{ uid }}" id="cited-s-low-{{ uid }}">
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Centred only when there is nothing to show. With reviews the heading sits
    above a grid and belongs on the left edge where the cards start; with none
    it was stranded at the far left of a 1700px section while the empty message
    sat centred a third of the way across, reading as two unrelated fragments
    with a lake between them.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if heading != blank -%}
    <h2 class="cited-reviews__heading{% unless has_reviews %} cited-reviews__heading--center{% endunless %}">
      {{ heading | escape }}
    </h2>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- if has_reviews and has_store -%}
    <div class="cited-tabs" role="tablist">
      <label class="cited-tabs__tab" for="cited-tab-product-{{ uid }}">Product Reviews</label>
      <label class="cited-tabs__tab" for="cited-tab-store-{{ uid }}">Store Reviews</label>
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    ── Compact summary, for the product title ──

    EMBED PATH ONLY. This exists to be lifted out of the section and dropped
    under the product title; the section below has its own full score and
    histogram. On the app-block path nothing moves it, so rendering it here
    would put two rating displays side by side in the same section — which is
    exactly what it did.

    A disclosure, not a panel that is always open — and `details`/`summary`
    rather than a popover driven by script. The block's whole premise is that
    it renders inside Shopify's own HTML response with no JavaScript, so a
    breakdown that needs JS to open would be invisible to exactly the crawlers
    this app exists to feed. The browser gives keyboard support, focus
    handling and Escape for free.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if has_reviews and is_embed -%}
    <details class="cited-rating" data-cited-summary style="{{ cited_vars }}">
      <summary class="cited-rating__trigger">
        <span class="cited-rating__value">{{ rating_avg | round: 1 }}</span>
        {%- render 'cited-stars', rating: rating_avg, scale_max: scale_max, size: 15 -%}
        <svg class="cited-rating__caret" viewBox="0 0 10 6" width="10" height="6" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M1 1l4 4 4-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
        </svg>
        <span class="cited-rating__count">
          {%- if rating_count == 1 -%}1 review{%- else -%}{{ rating_count }} reviews{%- endif -%}
        </span>
      </summary>

      <div class="cited-rating__panel">
        <p class="cited-rating__headline">
          {%- render 'cited-stars', rating: rating_avg, scale_max: scale_max, size: 16 -%}
          <strong>{{ rating_avg | round: 1 }} out of {{ scale_max }}</strong>
        </p>
        <p class="cited-rating__total">
          {{ rating_count }} global rating{% unless rating_count == 1 %}s{% endunless %}
        </p>

        {%- render 'cited-breakdown', breakdown: rating_breakdown, total: rating_count, scale_max: scale_max -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          The way out of the panel. Without it the breakdown is a dead end —
          a shopper who opens it has decided the average is not enough and
          wants to read what people actually wrote, and on the embed path the
          reviews themselves are a long way down the page.

          A plain in-page anchor, so it works with scripting off and the back
          button behaves.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <a class="cited-rating__more" href="#cited-reviews-{{ uid }}">
          See all {{ rating_count }} review{% unless rating_count == 1 %}s{% endunless %}
          <span aria-hidden="true">&rsaquo;</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </details>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    ── Reviews section ──

    Two columns: the verdict on the left, the evidence on the right. The left
    column is what a shopper checks before deciding whether to read any further
    — score, distribution, and the invitation to add their own — and it stays
    put while they read down the list.

    The compact disclosure above is lifted out of this section entirely on the
    embed path and dropped under the product title, so this header is not a
    duplicate of it: it is the only rating display left here once that move has
    happened.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    With no reviews there is nothing for the right-hand column to hold, so the
    two-column grid left a 14rem aside stranded against an empty half-page —
    "No reviews yet." broke one word per line inside it. The modifier collapses
    the whole thing to a single centred column for that case.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="cited-reviews__layout{% unless has_reviews %} cited-reviews__layout--blank{% endunless %}">
    <div class="cited-reviews__main">

  {%- comment -%}
    ── Summary: one line, not a column ──

    This was a 3rem score over a full histogram in a fixed 14–18rem aside,
    beside the reviews. Two things were wrong with that and both are
    structural rather than cosmetic.

    It pushed the reviews themselves below the fold of wherever the merchant
    put the block — the aggregate is context, and it was taking the space the
    evidence needed. And a fixed track cannot survive an unknown container:
    dropped in a product column it kept its 14rem while the review grid got
    what was left, which is how one review ended up marooned in a narrow strip
    beside half a page of nothing.

    A single line costs one row at any width, and the histogram becomes a
    disclosure for the shopper who wants it. Nothing is lost — the breakdown
    is still in the server's HTML, so it is still read by crawlers.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if has_reviews -%}
    <div class="cited-summary-bar">
      <p class="cited-summary-bar__score">
        {%- render 'cited-stars', rating: rating_avg, scale_max: scale_max, size: 16 -%}
        <strong class="cited-summary-bar__avg">{{ rating_avg | round: 1 }}</strong>
        <span class="cited-summary-bar__count">
          {{ rating_count }} review{% unless rating_count == 1 %}s{% endunless %}
        </span>
      </p>

      {%- if rating_breakdown != blank -%}
        <details class="cited-menu cited-summary-bar__more">
          <summary class="cited-chip cited-chip--menu">
            Rating breakdown
            <svg viewBox="0 0 10 6" width="10" height="6" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
              <path d="M1 1l4 4 4-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="cited-menu__panel cited-menu__panel--wide">
            {%- render 'cited-breakdown', breakdown: rating_breakdown, total: rating_count, scale_max: scale_max -%}
          </div>
        </details>
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        Signed out, this is a LINK to the login page rather than a form that
        rejects you after you have typed. Reviews require an account — a
        signed-out submission is unverifiable, which makes "verified purchase"
        meaningless — so the account step is unavoidable and saying so first is
        the honest order. `routes.account_login_url`, never a literal
        /account/login: that path 404s on new customer accounts and drops a
        shopper into the wrong locale on a translated store.

        Signed in, it jumps to the write form further down, which is a
        `details` the shopper opens themselves.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if enable_form and customer == blank -%}
        <a
          class="cited-reviews__write"
          {% if customer %}
            href="#cited-write-{{ uid }}"
          {% else %}
            href="{{ routes.account_login_url }}?return_url={{ product.url | url_encode }}"
          {% endif %}
        >
          {{ form_button | escape }}
        </a>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  {%- else -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      ── Empty state, as a designed state ──

      Every product on a new install is in this state, so it is the first thing
      a merchant sees after placing the block — and it used to be the aside's
      dashed box stranded in a narrow track. One centred column, the stars
      shown empty so the shape of what is coming is legible, and the
      invitation as the only action on offer.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="cited-blank">
      {%- render 'cited-stars', rating: 0, scale_max: scale_max, size: 20 -%}
      <p class="cited-blank__title">{{ empty_text | escape }}</p>
      {%- if enable_form and customer == blank -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          "Write a review", not `form_button`.

          `form_button` is the label on the button that SENDS the form — it
          defaults to "Submit review" — so using it here produced two identical
          black buttons stacked a few hundred pixels apart, one opening the
          form and one submitting it, with nothing to tell them apart. A
          shopper cannot act on that, and a merchant reads it as a duplicate.

          The default text is also not repeated underneath: `empty_text`
          already reads "No reviews yet. Be the first to share your thoughts.",
          and a second line saying the same thing in different words was the
          same invitation twice.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <a
          class="cited-reviews__write"
          {% if customer %}
            href="#cited-write-{{ uid }}"
          {% else %}
            href="{{ routes.account_login_url }}?return_url={{ product.url | url_encode }}"
          {% endif %}
        >
          Write a review
        </a>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    ── Images from reviews ──

    Every photo across the reviews on this page, in one scrollable strip. It is
    the first thing a shopper looks at — a product worn by someone who is not a
    model answers "what does this actually look like" faster than any
    paragraph — and it was previously buried, four or five photos at a time,
    inside individual reviews a shopper had to scroll to find.

    Each photo is an anchor to the review it came from, so the strip is a way
    INTO the reviews rather than a lightbox that dead-ends. No arrows: a
    button that scrolls a container needs JavaScript, and native overflow
    scrolling with snap points already works by trackpad, touch, scrollbar and
    keyboard. Arrows drawn for looks that did nothing without JS would be worse
    than none.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if gallery != blank and gallery.size > 1 -%}
    <div class="cited-gallery">
      <p class="cited-gallery__head">
        Images from reviews
        <span class="cited-gallery__count">{{ gallery.size }} photo{% unless gallery.size == 1 %}s{% endunless %}</span>
      </p>
      <ul class="cited-gallery__strip" role="list">
        {%- for media_url in gallery limit: 16 -%}
          <li class="cited-gallery__item">
            <a class="cited-gallery__link" href="#cited-reviews-{{ uid }}">
              <img
                src="{{ media_url }}"
                alt="Photo from a customer review"
                loading="lazy"
                decoding="async"
                width="112"
                height="140"
              >
            </a>
          </li>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </ul>
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    ── Filter and sort ──

    Labels only. Every one of them drives a radio declared at the top of the
    section, so this whole row is inert markup — nothing here needs scripting,
    and it all still works with JS disabled.

    A chip is rendered only when it would match something. "Review with videos"
    on a product whose reviews are all text is a control whose only possible
    outcome is an empty list, and a shopper who clicks it concludes the widget
    is broken rather than that the filter worked.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if has_reviews and reviews_list != blank -%}
    <div class="cited-toolbar">
      <div class="cited-chips">
        {%- comment -%}
          Each chip names itself in a class rather than relying on its position.
          `nth-of-type` would have worked only while all three are present: on a
          product whose reviews have video but no photos, the video chip becomes
          the second label and a positional rule highlights the wrong one — or
          nothing at all.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <label class="cited-chip cited-chip--all" for="cited-f-all-{{ uid }}">All</label>
        {%- if any_photos -%}
          <label class="cited-chip cited-chip--photo" for="cited-f-photo-{{ uid }}">Review with photos</label>
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- if any_videos -%}
          <label class="cited-chip cited-chip--video" for="cited-f-video-{{ uid }}">Review with videos</label>
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          `details` for the rating menu, because a dropdown that opens is the
          one control here a browser gives us for free — keyboard, Escape and
          focus handling included. The options inside are labels for radios
          declared outside it, which is what lets a menu item filter a list it
          is not a sibling of.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <details class="cited-menu">
          <summary class="cited-chip cited-chip--menu">
            All ratings
            <svg viewBox="0 0 10 6" width="10" height="6" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
              <path d="M1 1l4 4 4-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="cited-menu__panel">
            <label class="cited-menu__item" for="cited-r-all-{{ uid }}">All ratings</label>
            {%- for i in (1..5) reversed -%}
              <label class="cited-menu__item" for="cited-r-{{ i }}-{{ uid }}">
                {{ i }} star{% unless i == 1 %}s{% endunless %}
              </label>
            {%- endfor -%}
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <div class="cited-sort">
        <span class="cited-sort__label">Sort by</span>
        <details class="cited-menu cited-menu--right">
          <summary class="cited-chip cited-chip--menu">
            <span class="cited-sort__current">Newest</span>
            <svg viewBox="0 0 10 6" width="10" height="6" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
              <path d="M1 1l4 4 4-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
            </svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="cited-menu__panel">
            <label class="cited-menu__item" for="cited-s-new-{{ uid }}">Newest</label>
            <label class="cited-menu__item" for="cited-s-high-{{ uid }}">Highest rated</label>
            <label class="cited-menu__item" for="cited-s-low-{{ uid }}">Lowest rated</label>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    ── AI summary ──
    Sits between the aggregate and the review list because that is the order a
    shopper reads in: how many liked it, what they said about it, then the
    reviews themselves. Renders nothing when the metafield is absent.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if has_reviews and review_summary != blank -%}
    {%- render 'cited-summary', summary: review_summary, uid: uid -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    ── Review list ──
    Rendered server-side. Only the first page is emitted inline; further pages
    are fetched by the enhancement script. The first page is what matters for
    SEO and AI ingestion, so crawlability never depends on JS.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if reviews_list != blank -%}
    <ol class="cited-reviews__list cited-reviews__list--{{ list_bucket }}" role="list">
      {%- for review in reviews_list limit: per_page -%}
        {%- liquid
          assign r_rating = 0
          if review.rating != blank and review.rating.value != blank
            assign r_rating = review.rating.value.rating | times: 1.0
          endif
          # `author` on the standard definition is a customer reference, not a
          # name. The display name lives in `author_display_name`.
          assign r_author = review.author_display_name | default: anonymous_label
          assign r_verified = false
          if review.app_verification_status == 'verified_buyer'
            assign r_verified = true
          endif

          # Whole stars, for the rating filter and for the sort. `round` on a
          # 4.5 gives 5, which is the bucket a shopper filtering for "5 stars"
          # expects a 4.5 to be in.
          assign r_int = r_rating | round
          if r_int < 1
            assign r_int = 1
          endif

          # What kind of media this review carries, decided here rather than in
          # the markup below so the card can be classed before it is drawn —
          # the photo and video filters are plain CSS over these classes.
          assign r_photo = false
          assign r_video = false
          for media_url in review.media_urls
            if media_url contains '/cdn/shop/videos/' or media_url contains '.mp4' or media_url contains '.webm' or media_url contains '.mov'
              assign r_video = true
            else
              assign r_photo = true
            endif
          endfor
        -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          `order` is what makes sorting work without script. Both values are
          stamped per card and the stylesheet picks one; grid honours `order`,
          so "highest rated" is 5 - rating and "lowest" is rating, with the
          server's newest-first order preserved inside each rating bucket.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <li
          class="cited-review cited-review--r{{ r_int }}{% if r_photo %} cited-review--photo{% endif %}{% if r_video %} cited-review--video{% endif %}"
          style="--cited-rank-high: {{ 5 | minus: r_int }}; --cited-rank-low: {{ r_int }};"
        >
          {%- comment -%}
            Reading order: verdict → headline → argument → evidence → who said
            it → whether they bought it → when.

            The rating leads because it is the one fact every card has and the
            one a shopper sorts on by eye; the person arrives at the end, where
            "and this was a verified buyer, last month" is the thing that
            settles it. The previous order opened with a name and a date — the
            two facts a stranger's name tells you least — and a grid of those
            reads as a list of people rather than a body of evidence.

            The identity block is a footer, so it sits below content of
            unpredictable length and every card still ends the same way.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <div class="cited-review__head">
            {%- render 'cited-stars', rating: r_rating, scale_max: scale_max, size: 15 -%}
          </div>

          {%- if review.title != blank -%}
            <h3 class="cited-review__title">{{ review.title | escape }}</h3>
          {%- endif -%}

          {%- if review.body != blank -%}
            <div class="cited-review__body">{{ review.body | escape | newline_to_br }}</div>
          {%- endif -%}

          {%- comment -%}
            `media_urls` is the ONLY media field on the standard product_review
            definition — a bare list of URLs, with no type discriminator, and
            we cannot add a field to a definition Shopify owns. So the type is
            inferred from the URL, which is why the check below is broader than
            a file extension: Shopify's transcoded sources live under
            /cdn/shop/videos/ and carry a rendition suffix before the .mp4.

            Getting this wrong is visible rather than silent — a video URL in
            an <img> is a broken image — which is the failure mode to prefer
            over guessing wrong in the other direction.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- if show_media and review.media_urls != blank -%}
            <div class="cited-review__media">
              {%- for media_url in review.media_urls limit: 5 -%}
                {%- liquid
                  assign is_video = false
                  if media_url contains '/cdn/shop/videos/'
                    assign is_video = true
                  elsif media_url contains '.mp4'
                    assign is_video = true
                  elsif media_url contains '.webm'
                    assign is_video = true
                  elsif media_url contains '.mov'
                    assign is_video = true
                  endif
                -%}
                {%- if is_video -%}
                  {%- comment -%}
                    No poster attribute: the standard field gives us one URL per
                    item, so there is no thumbnail to pair with this source.
                    `#t=0.1` asks the browser for the frame a tenth of a second
                    in, which is what makes the player show the clip instead of
                    a black rectangle. `preload="metadata"` keeps that cheap —
                    a review list must not pull down several megabytes of video
                    nobody pressed play on.
                  {%- endcomment -%}
                  <video
                    class="cited-review__media-item cited-review__media-item--video"
                    src="{{ media_url }}#t=0.1"
                    controls
                    playsinline
                    preload="metadata"
                    aria-label="Customer video from a review by {{ r_author | escape }}"
                  ></video>
                {%- else -%}
                  <img
                    class="cited-review__media-item"
                    src="{{ media_url }}"
                    alt="Customer photo from a review by {{ r_author | escape }}"
                    loading="lazy"
                    decoding="async"
                    width="88"
                    height="88"
                  >
                {%- endif -%}
              {%- endfor -%}
            </div>
          {%- endif -%}

          {%- comment -%}
            Who said it, and whether they bought it — a footer, not a header.
            It sits after content of unpredictable length so every card in a
            row still ends the same way, and `margin-top: auto` in the CSS
            pins it to the bottom of a card that is shorter than its
            neighbours.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <footer class="cited-review__meta">
            <span class="cited-review__author">{{ r_author | escape }}</span>
            {%- comment -%}
              This is OUR order-match badge. It is deliberately NOT, and must
              never resemble, the "Verified by Shop" badge.

              Shopify's rule for approved review apps: use only the official
              badge assets it provides, and "do not modify, recreate, or alter
              the badge in any way." The two marks also mean different things —
              "Verified by Shop" is Shopify attesting via the Shop app, while
              this says only that Cited matched the reviewer to an order for
              this product on this store.

              So it is kept distinct on every axis a reviewer would check:
                * mark    — a bag outline, not a checkmark. A check inside a
                            circle is the shape people read as Shopify's.
                * colour  — green, distinct from all three official variants
                            (purple, black, grey).
                * wording — names the store, not Shop.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- if r_verified -%}
              <span class="cited-review__verified" title="Cited matched this reviewer to an order on this store">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="12" height="12" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                  <path
                    fill="none"
                    stroke="currentColor"
                    stroke-width="1.4"
                    stroke-linejoin="round"
                    d="M3.4 5.6h9.2l-.8 7.3a1 1 0 0 1-1 .9H5.2a1 1 0 0 1-1-.9zM6 5.6V4.3a2 2 0 0 1 4 0v1.3"
                  />
                </svg>
                Verified purchase
              </span>
            {%- endif -%}

            {%- if review.submitted_at != blank -%}
              <time class="cited-review__date" datetime="{{ review.submitted_at | date: '%Y-%m-%d' }}">
                {%- comment -%}
                  An explicit strftime, not `format: 'date'`. Named formats
                  resolve against the THEME's locale file, and a theme that
                  does not define `date_formats.date` — most do not — makes
                  the filter a no-op that prints the raw stored value.
                  Shoppers were being shown "2026-08-19T20:55:28Z".
                {%- endcomment -%}
                {{ review.submitted_at | date: '%d %B %Y' }}
              </time>
            {%- endif -%}
          </footer>

          {%- if review.merchant_reply != blank -%}
            <div class="cited-review__reply">
              <span class="cited-review__reply-label">{{ shop.name | escape }} replied</span>
              <p>{{ review.merchant_reply | escape | newline_to_br }}</p>
            </div>
          {%- endif -%}
        </li>
      {%- endfor -%}
    </ol>

    {%- if rating_count > per_page -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Enhancement target. Server-rendered as a plain link to the product
        page so it still works with JS disabled; the script upgrades it to
        in-place pagination.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <a
        class="cited-reviews__more"
        href="{{ product.url }}#cited-reviews-{{ uid }}"
        data-cited-more
      >
        {{ load_more_text | escape }}
      </a>
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    ── Write a review ──

    A plain HTML form posting to the app proxy on this shop's own domain. No
    JavaScript, no iframe, no third-party origin: it submits, the server
    answers with a rendered page in this theme, and it behaves identically
    with scripting disabled. The proxy path must match `subpath` in
    shopify.app.toml — Liquid cannot read that file.

    A signed-in shopper's name and email are prefilled from their account, and
    only that path can earn a "Verified purchase" badge: the app looks the
    address up from Shopify rather than believing this form. A typed address
    is an unauthenticated claim and is treated as one.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Only for a signed-in shopper. Signed out, the invitation lives in the aside
    above and is a link to the login page — saying so before any effort is
    invested beats rejecting a filled-in form.

    A `details` rather than a form that is always open: the write form is the
    largest thing in the block and almost every visitor is here to read, not to
    write. Collapsed by default it stops dominating a section whose job is to
    show what other people said. `open` is not set, so the server's HTML is the
    collapsed state and no layout shift happens on load.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if enable_form and customer -%}
    <details class="cited-reviews__form-wrap" id="cited-write-{{ uid }}">
      <summary class="cited-reviews__form-heading">{{ form_heading | escape }}</summary>

      <form
        class="cited-reviews__form"
        method="post"
        action="/apps/cited/reviews"
        accept-charset="UTF-8"
        enctype="multipart/form-data"
        data-cited-form
      >
        <input type="hidden" name="product_id" value="{{ product.id }}">
        <input type="hidden" name="return_path" value="{{ product.url }}">

        {%- comment -%}
          ── Rating ──

          Real stars the shopper clicks, built from a radio group rather than
          script: the inputs are visually hidden but still focusable, and each
          label is the star itself. `row-reverse` puts 1 on the left while the
          DOM runs 5→1, which is what lets `input:checked ~ label` light up the
          chosen star and every one below it with no JavaScript at all.

          A click-to-rate strip driven by JS would look the same and submit
          nothing with scripting off.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <fieldset class="cited-rate">
          <legend class="cited-rate__legend">Your rating</legend>
          <div class="cited-rate__stars">
            {%- for offset in (1..5) -%}
              {%- assign value = 6 | minus: offset -%}
              <input
                class="cited-rate__input"
                type="radio"
                id="cited-rate-{{ uid }}-{{ value }}"
                name="rating"
                value="{{ value }}"
                required
              >
              <label class="cited-rate__label" for="cited-rate-{{ uid }}-{{ value }}">
                <span class="cited-rate__sr">{{ value }} star{% if value != 1 %}s{% endif %}</span>
                <svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" width="26" height="26" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                  <path d="M10 1.6l2.6 5.2 5.8.85-4.2 4.1 1 5.75L10 14.8l-5.2 2.7 1-5.75L1.6 7.65l5.8-.85z"/>
                </svg>
              </label>
            {%- endfor -%}
          </div>
        </fieldset>

        {%- comment -%}
          One box, not two. A separate title field asks the shopper to
          summarise something they have not written yet, and it is the field
          people leave blank or fill with "Good".
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <label class="cited-reviews__field">
          <span class="cited-reviews__field-label">Your review</span>
          <textarea
            name="body"
            rows="3"
            maxlength="5000"
            required
            placeholder="What did you think? How did it work out for you?"
          ></textarea>
        </label>

        {%- comment -%}
          Photos are optional and go to the merchant's own Shopify Files. The
          input accepts images only and is capped server-side at four; the
          attribute here is a courtesy to the file picker, not the limit.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if show_media -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            The native file input renders as "Browse… No files selected" — an
            OS-styled control that ignores every theme setting, spans whatever
            width it likes and reads as a form from 1998 next to the rest of
            this block.

            So the input is visually hidden but left in the DOM, focusable and
            fully functional: it is a real <input type="file"> inside its
            <label>, so clicking the tile opens the picker with no JavaScript
            and keyboard users tab straight to it. The tile is decoration over
            a working control, not a replacement for one.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <label class="cited-reviews__field cited-reviews__photos">
            <span class="cited-reviews__field-label">Photos <span class="cited-reviews__optional">optional</span></span>
            <span class="cited-upload">
              <input
                class="cited-upload__input"
                type="file"
                name="photos"
                accept="image/jpeg,image/png,image/webp,image/gif"
                multiple
              >
              <span class="cited-upload__tile">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                  <rect x="3" y="5" width="18" height="14" rx="2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/>
                  <circle cx="8.5" cy="10" r="1.6" fill="currentColor"/>
                  <path d="M4 17l4.5-4.5 3 3L15 12l5 5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                </svg>
              </span>
              <small class="cited-upload__hint">Up to 4 images, 5 MB each.</small>
            </span>
          </label>

          {%- comment -%}
            One video, separate input. A single `accept` list mixing images and
            video would let someone pick four clips and have three silently
            dropped server-side, which reads as the upload being broken.

            `video/quicktime` is not optional: it is what iOS sends for a .mov,
            so leaving it out rejects most phone uploads.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- if video_enabled -%}
          <label class="cited-reviews__field cited-reviews__video">
            <span class="cited-reviews__field-label">Video <span class="cited-reviews__optional">optional</span></span>
            <span class="cited-upload">
              <input
                class="cited-upload__input"
                type="file"
                name="video"
                accept="video/mp4,video/quicktime,video/webm"
              >
              <span class="cited-upload__tile">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                  <rect x="3" y="6" width="12" height="12" rx="2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/>
                  <path d="M15 10.5l5-2.5v8l-5-2.5z" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                </svg>
              </span>
              <small class="cited-upload__hint">One video, up to 50 MB. It may take a minute to appear.</small>
            </span>
          </label>
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          The shopper is never asked who they are. Shopify already knows, and
          the app resolves the name and address server-side from the customer
          id the proxy signs — which is the only version of it worth trusting.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          Only when there is actually a name to show. A Shopify customer can
          have neither first_name nor name — an account created at checkout
          with just an email has both blank — and this rendered as a dangling
          "Posting as" with nothing after it, which reads like a bug to the
          shopper and tells them nothing either way.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- assign posting_as = customer.first_name | default: customer.name -%}
        {%- if posting_as != blank -%}
          <p class="cited-reviews__as">
            Posting as <strong>{{ posting_as | escape }}</strong>
          </p>
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- comment -%}
          Honeypot. Hidden from people by CSS and from screen readers by
          aria-hidden, left visible to the sort of bot that fills every input
          it can find. Rejected server-side.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div class="cited-reviews__hp" aria-hidden="true">
          <label>Website<input type="text" name="website" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off"></label>
        </div>

        <div class="cited-reviews__actions">
          <button class="cited-reviews__submit" type="submit">{{ form_button | escape }}</button>
          <p class="cited-reviews__status" data-cited-status role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
        </div>
      </form>
    </details>
  {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    ── Store Reviews ──

    The second tab: what people say about this shop, not this product. It reads
    from the store-wide digest the worker already publishes for the carousel and
    testimonial blocks, and renders through the same card snippet those use — a
    second card written here would be the drift that made the two entry points
    of this very file diverge.

    A sibling of the product layout, and of the radios at the top, which is what
    lets one CSS rule swap them. Rendered only when the digest has something in
    it, so the tab bar never opens an empty panel.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if has_reviews and has_store -%}
    <div class="cited-store">
      <ul class="cited-store__grid" role="list">
        {%- for r in store_reviews limit: 9 -%}
          <li>
            {%- render 'cited-quote-card', r: r, scale_max: scale_max, anon: anonymous_label, show_product: true -%}
          </li>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </ul>
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}
</section>

{%- comment -%}
  Submit without a reload. Loaded only when the form is on the page, and
  purely additive: the form works as a plain POST without it.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if enable_form and customer -%}
  <script src="{{ 'cited-reviews-form.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  ── Structured data ──
  Emitted only when reviews actually exist. Emitting AggregateRating with zero
  reviews is a Google structured-data violation and a fast route to a manual
  action — which for a merchant means a 1-star app review (PLAN.md §5.4.5).

  @id is pinned to the canonical product URL so search engines MERGE this with
  the theme's existing Product node rather than treating it as a second,
  competing product entity.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if has_reviews and enable_jsonld -%}
  {%- capture cited_jsonld -%}
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Product",
    "@id": {{ shop.url | append: product.url | json }},
    "name": {{ product.title | json }},
    "aggregateRating": {
      "@type": "AggregateRating",
      "ratingValue": {{ rating_avg | round: 2 | json }},
      "reviewCount": {{ rating_count | json }},
      "bestRating": {{ scale_max | json }},
      "worstRating": 1
    }
    {%- if reviews_list != blank -%}
    ,"review": [
      {%- for review in reviews_list limit: 5 -%}
        {%- liquid
          assign r_rating = 0
          if review.rating != blank and review.rating.value != blank
            assign r_rating = review.rating.value.rating | times: 1.0
          endif
        -%}
        {
          "@type": "Review",
          "reviewRating": {
            "@type": "Rating",
            "ratingValue": {{ r_rating | round: 1 | json }},
            "bestRating": {{ scale_max | json }},
            "worstRating": 1
          },
          "author": {
            "@type": "Person",
            "name": {{ review.author_display_name | default: 'Anonymous' | json }}
          }
          {%- if review.body != blank -%}
          ,"reviewBody": {{ review.body | json }}
          {%- endif -%}
          {%- if review.submitted_at != blank -%}
          ,"datePublished": {{ review.submitted_at | date: '%Y-%m-%d' | json }}
          {%- endif -%}
        }{%- unless forloop.last -%},{%- endunless -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    ]
    {%- endif -%}
  }
  {%- endcapture -%}
  <script type="application/ld+json">{{ cited_jsonld }}</script>
{%- endif -%}
